# SPARQL

```elixir
Mix.install(rudof_elixir: [path: Path.expand("../..", __DIR__)], kino: "== 0.19.0")
```

## Introduction

This document contains a short introduction to [SPARQL](https://www.w3.org/TR/sparql11-query/) using [rudof](https://rudof-project.github.io/).

## Preliminaries

The library is available as `rudof_elixir` from this checkout. The main entry point is the `Rudof.Session` module, through which most of the functionality is provided. `Session.new!/1` accepts a TOML string for session customization.

```elixir
alias Rudof.Session
import ExUnit.Assertions
rudof = Session.new!()
```

## SPARQL queries with local RDF data

As SPARQL is an RDF query language, we first need some RDF data.

rudof can run queries against local RDF data or RDF that is available through some SPARQL endpoint.

Let's start with some local RDF data.

```elixir
rdf = ~S"""
prefix :    <http://example.org/>
prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#>

:alice a :Person ;
 :name      "Alice"                ;
 :birthDate "2005-03-01"^^xsd:date ;
 :worksFor  :acme                   .
:bob a :Person   ;
 :name      "Robert Smith"         ;
 :birthDate "2003-01-02"^^xsd:date ;
 :worksFor  :acme  .
:acme a :Company ;
 :name "Acme Inc." .
"""

:ok = Session.read_data(rudof, rdf)
```

We can run SPARQL queries as follows:

```elixir
query = ~S"""
PREFIX : <http://example.org/>

SELECT ?person ?name ?date ?company WHERE {
  ?person a          :Person ;
          :name      ?name   ;
          :birthDate ?date   ;
          :worksFor  ?c   .
  ?c      :name      ?company .
}
"""

{:ok, results} = Session.query(rudof, query)
assert length(results.rows) == 2
Kino.DataTable.new(Rudof.Visualization.table_data(results))
```

Show the results:

```elixir
Kino.DataTable.new(Rudof.Visualization.table_data(results))
```

Reset the local session so the following examples start with a clean dataset:

```elixir
:ok = Session.reset(rudof)
```

## SPARQL queries with an endpoint

It is also possible to run SPARQL queries against SPARQL endpoints. SPARQL endpoints are usually identified by an IRI. rudof contains a list of popular SPARQL endpoints which can also be identified by name, like wikidata, whose IRI is: https://query.wikidata.org/sparql

We indicate rudof that we will use Wikidata's endpoint:

This section calls Wikidata directly. `Session.read_endpoint/2` attaches the endpoint as the native data backend, and the SPARQL query runs through Rust. Evaluating this section needs network access; the endpoint and returned results are controlled by Wikidata.

```elixir
wikidata_endpoint = "https://query.wikidata.org/sparql"

Session.with_session(fn remote ->
  :ok = Session.read_endpoint(remote, wikidata_endpoint)

  {:ok, results} =
    Session.query(
      remote,
      "PREFIX wd: <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/>\nPREFIX wdt: <http://www.wikidata.org/prop/direct/>\nPREFIX rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#>\nSELECT ?person ?occupation WHERE {\n  ?p wdt:P31 wd:Q5 ; wdt:P106 ?o ; rdfs:label ?person ; wdt:P19 wd:Q14317 .\n  ?o rdfs:label ?occupation\n  FILTER (lang(?person) = \"en\" && lang(?occupation) = \"en\")\n} LIMIT 10\n"
    )

  Kino.DataTable.new(Rudof.Visualization.table_data(results))
end)
```

